W4A8 on the whole array: int4 weights make the int8 GEMM 1.23-1.26x faster at its best tile -- through bytes, not MACs
=========================================================================================================

Desktop 2 (DESKTOP-CBL5NUA; Ryzen 7 8700G, Phoenix XDNA1, Windows 11), 2026-09-10: compile-only
builds from 14:07, NPU runs 14:11:51-14:19:47,
mlir-aie v1.4.2 ironenv (Peano llvm-aie 22), XRT 2.21.0, NPU driver 32.0.20101.3760, power mode
Default. whole_array's 4 x 4 cores (4 columns). Design, kernels and tools: kernels/w4a8_array/.
Raw rows: results/aie/w4a8_array_raw.jsonl. Witness: results/aie/witness_w4a8_array.jsonl.

QUESTION
--------
The one-core probe (results/aie/w4a8_probe_npu.log) found int8 x int4 to be a native AIE2 vmac
(a k loop 1.82x int8's on one core) and int4->int8 widening on load to be free but MAC-neutral.
It left the array open: does W4A8 speed up whole_array's int8 GEMM, and through which half --
the MACs (the native kernel) or the bytes (B at half the size through shim, memtile and core
DMA)? It bounded the MAC half at <= ~1.18x (DERIVED), assuming everything outside the kernel
stayed fixed.

ANSWER
------
1. At the int8 GEMM's best tile -- 64/128/64 at 2048^3, C single-buffered, the tile behind the
   repo's 4852.06 GOPS -- int4 B makes the array 1.23-1.26x faster, bit-exact. native:unroll2
   runs 6,195.33 GOPS (mean of 3 processes) against this sitting's upstream int8 4,906.23: the
   highest whole_array rate measured in this repo (op count 2MKN as for every other row; the
   operands are int8 x int4). Every int4 arm lands in the same place -- unpack:unroll2 1.241x,
   unpack:no-unroll 1.234x, native:default 1.231x, native:unroll2 1.263x -- and the int4 arms'
   ranges overlap each other while each is disjoint from every int8 run.

2. At that tile the gain is B's bytes, not the MAC rate. The unpack arms multiply at int8's
   rate and gain as much as the native ones. A faster int8 kernel gains nothing: i8:unroll2,
   176 fewer cycles per call than i8:default on one core, runs 0.995x. So the kernel is not on
   the critical path there, and the probe's "<= ~1.18x" -- a bound on the kernel half -- is
   SUPERSEDED in the wrong direction: the kernel half is ~0 and the bytes half is ~1.24x. Mean
   int4 time is 0.8035 of upstream's; packing B removes 20 % of the design's L3 bytes
   (80 -> 64 MiB), a 0.800 prediction written before the run (below).

3. The gain depends on the tile, and "time follows L3 bytes" does not generalize:
     tile        unpack     native     int8 re-typed   (x upstream, same tile, same sitting)
     64/128/64   1.241x     1.263x     1.008x
     64/64/64    1.057x     1.127x     1.015x
     128/64/64   0.971x     1.015x     1.016x
   At 64/64/64 native beats unpack by 6.7 % with disjoint ranges: there the MAC rate shows. At
   128/64/64 native's 1.015x is inside upstream's own spread (not resolved), and unpack's 0.971x
   is resolved -- all three unpack runs sit above every upstream and i8 run -- observed, not
   explained.

4. The mechanism is UNEXPLAINED. Ruled out, each by a pair of measured rows:
   - the core kernel: i8:unroll2 does not move at 64/128/64, and native's doubled MAC rate adds
     nothing over unpack there;
   - total L3 bytes at a fixed bandwidth: the int4 arms at 64/128/64 and upstream int8 at
     128/64/64 both move 64 MiB, in 2,773-2,846 us against 3,614 us; upstream int8 alone runs
     23.96 / 21.46 / 18.57 GB/s of L3 traffic at 64/128/64 / 64/64/64 / 128/64/64;
   - bytes into each core per call (docs/SILICON.md 3.1's bytes-per-MAC model): those same two
     configurations deliver 8 KB of A and 4 KB of B per 524,288-MAC call, the same 0.0234 B/MAC,
     and take 4,874-5,002 against 6,353 cycles per call (DERIVED at 1.80 GHz).
   That pair also differs in k (128 vs 64), A's contiguous DMA run (128 vs 64 B), B's run
   (32 vs 64 B) and B's L3 re-stream count (8 vs 4), so it does not say which of those carries
   the effect, and 64/64/64 (m as the first tile, k as the second) lands in between: the effect
   tracks neither m nor k alone.

5. The capacity half bought nothing here. Halving B lets 64/128/64 double-buffer C (L1 60,672 B;
   int8 needs 68,864 B and the allocator refused it, section A): native:unroll2 C x2 runs 1.255x
   against 1.263x single-buffered, ranges overlapping.

6. The data path itself works: B packed two per byte through shim -> memtile -> core, with the
   memtile->core transform's innermost dimension one 32-bit word (int8's is two), lowered,
   ran, and matched numpy exactly at all three tiles -- all 49 runs (the pilot and the three
   sweeps' 48), every one returning the same C (sha256 e426d437887b5f12).

7. H11's reading gets a second, independent confirmation: the core is not the critical path of
   this design at its best tile. H11 saw a 12.5 % static kernel improvement vanish; here a
   measured 4.7 % per-call improvement (i8:unroll2's 3,559 against 3,735 cycles) vanishes too.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT ESTABLISH
----------------------------
- One shape (2048^3), three tiles, whole_array's 4 x 4 cores, 3 processes per arm x 10 timed
  iterations (3 warm-up), as the tile sweep ran. Run-to-run spread of the per-process averages
  is 2-4 %; the table marks as unresolved every arm whose range overlaps upstream's.
- Nothing about a W4 network's accuracy: this is the GEMM, not a quantization recipe.
- The cycles per call are DERIVED at 1.80 GHz from the NPU bracket; nothing was traced here.
- The unexplained mechanism in 4 is not a claim that no model fits: only that the three
  obvious ones do not, and that the tile pair which would separate the rest was not run.
- The upstream arm reproduces its tile-sweep anchors within the day-to-day drift this machine
  shows (4,906.23 against 4,852.06 at 64/128/64; 4,753.23 against 4,683.89 at 128/64/64), but
  every ratio above is against this sitting's upstream, never the anchor.
- The witness samples once a second and each run holds a context for well under a second of
  NPU time, so 10 of its 526 samples caught one; a foreign context shorter than a second could
  slip between samples. xrt-smi read "No hardware contexts running" at the start and the end
  of every block, and the one other session using the NPU today had confirmed its block over.

METHOD
------
Design: kernels/w4a8_array/whole_array_w4a8.py, a copy of mlir-aie v1.4.2's whole_array.py by
way of kernels/bank_placement/whole_array_bankpad.py (--c-single-buffer, --stack-size), cut to
int8 A / int32 C / row-major B and C. The fifo topology, core loop, tensor tilers and runtime
sequence are upstream's; only the kernel and B's byte geometry vary per arm:
  upstream  kernels.mm(): upstream mm.cc's matmul_i8_i32 + zero_i32 -- whole_array as built
  i8        mm_i8i8_local (upstream's kernel re-typed; the one-core probe's control), B int8
  unpack    mm_i8i4_unpack, B packed int4 widened on load, int8 mmul
  native    mm_i8i4_native, B packed int4, aie::mmul<4,16,8,int8,int4>
The last three come from kernels/w4a8_array/w4a8_array_kernels.cc, which #includes the probe's
kernels/w4a8_probe/w4a8_kernels.cc unchanged and takes zero_i32 from upstream's own zero.cc.
Packed B is K x N/2 bytes, two's complement, column 2j in the low nibble -- the layout the probe
verified -- and every B dimension of the design is in bytes (the memtile->core transform emits
s x t int4 blocks as s rows of t/2 bytes). Every arm draws the same A (int8, full range, seed
1726250518) and the same B values (in [-8, 7], for the int8 arms too), so every arm must
return the same C; each run checks it against a float64 numpy product (exact here) and prints
its sha256. Timing is whole_array's own: aie.utils.benchmark.run_iters around the design, the
NPU bracket from the runtime's timer around kernel.wait(), --warmup 3 --iters 10, GOPS = 2MKN
over the average. kernels/w4a8_array/sweep.py runs one fresh process per (arm, repetition),
rotating the arm order by one place per repetition; table.py builds section G from the raw rows.

Checks, in order: compile-only (AOT) builds of every arm to test the packed-B lowering and the
L1 arithmetic before any device use (section A); object_check.py (section B) compiles each
arm's kernel with IRON's exact Peano command and compares the function, bundle for bundle, with
(i) the one-core probe's compile of the same source and defines and (ii) the object the IRON
JIT actually linked -- identical everywhere, so the probe's loop table and section B describe
what ran; one pilot run of the riskiest arm (packed B through the new 4-byte transform) before
the sweep, since overlapping memtile patterns have hung this device before
(results/aie/im2col_bd_probe_npu.log); the predictions written down before each block.

Gates: xrt-smi "No hardware contexts running on device" before the block and at the start and
end of each sweep; tools/host_load.ps1 CLEAR (0.26 busy cores of 16, 17.3 GB free); the other
session that had used the NPU earlier today confirmed its block over before this one began.

PREDICTION (written 2026-09-10, before any NPU run of this design)
-----------------------------------------------------------------
Geometry: 2048^3, tile m/k/n = 64/128/64, 4 x 4 cores, C single-buffered unless marked.

L3 traffic per GEMM, from the design's own tilers (DERIVED): A's 64 x 2048 row strips are
streamed once per column block of the array, N/(4n) = 8 times, so A = 32 MiB; B's column
strips once per row block, M/(4m) = 8 times, so B = 32 MiB int8 / 16 MiB int4; C = 16 MiB
(int32, once). Total 80 MiB int8, 64 MiB int4: B is 40 % of the bytes, and packing it saves
20 % of all L3 traffic. At the anchor's 3,540.7 us that is 23.7 GB/s of L3 traffic.

Per core call (DERIVED from the anchor, results/aie/gemm_tile_sweep_c_single_buffer_npu.log):
3,540.7 us x 1.80 GHz / 1,024 calls per core = 6,224 cycles. One-core kernel cycles per
call at 64/128/64 (MEASURED, results/aie/w4a8_probe_npu.log section E): i8:default 3,735,
i8:unroll2 3,559, unpack:unroll2 3,749, unpack:no-unroll 3,509, native:default 3,202,
native:unroll2 2,791. Upstream's own matmul_i8_i32 was never timed alone; its loop is
i8:default's length, 8 vmacs and one A&A pair, so take 3,735 for it. That leaves 2,489
cycles per call outside the kernel.

Three models of that 2,489, and what each predicts (speedup over upstream, same sitting):

  arm                  kernel   (a) fixed      (b) additive, non-kernel   (c) overlapped:
                       cyc/call     non-kernel     x bytes (0.8 for int4)     max(kernel, DMA)
  upstream / i8:default  3,735     1.000          1.000                      1.000
  i8:unroll2             3,559     1.029          1.029                      1.000
  unpack:unroll2         3,749     0.998          1.084                      1.250
  unpack:no-unroll       3,509     1.038          1.132                      1.250
  native:default         3,202     1.094          1.199                      1.250
  native:unroll2         2,791     1.179          1.302                      1.250

  (c) assumes the anchor is DMA-bound at 6,224 cycles per call, so int8 arms all tie and
  every int4 arm lands at 6,224 x 0.8 = 4,979 cycles whatever its kernel.

What separates them: i8:unroll2 vs i8:default (does a faster int8 kernel show at all?
yes under a/b, no under c); unpack vs its int8 partner (bytes only: nothing under a, 8-13 %
under b, 25 % under c); native vs unpack (the MAC effect: present under a/b, absent under
c). native:unroll2 with C double-buffered (L1 60,672 B, which int8 cannot fit: 68,864 B,
confirmed by the allocator in the compile-only pass) tests whether the single-buffered C
tile's drain -- 16 KB per output tile, about 256 cycles per call at 4 B/cycle if it
serializes -- is part of the 2,489.

None of these is a measurement. The anchor is from 2026-09-07/08; this sitting's upstream
arm replaces it as the denominator.

ADDENDUM, written after the 64/128/64 sweep and before any 128/64/64 run
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
The 64/128/64 sweep came out as model (c) predicted in shape: no int8 kernel change showed
(i8:unroll2 0.995x), and every int4 arm, unpack included, landed at 1.23-1.26x, mean int4 time
0.8035 of upstream's against the 0.800 the L3 byte count gives. A second tile tests whether
"time follows L3 bytes" is a law or a coincidence of one geometry.

At 128/64/64, 2048^3 (DERIVED from the same tilers): A row strips streamed N/(4n) = 8 times
= 32 MiB; B column strips streamed M/(4m) = 4 times = 16 MiB int8 / 8 MiB int4; C 16 MiB.
Total 64 MiB int8, 56 MiB int4. Predictions for int4 over int8 at this tile:
  time follows total L3 bytes (reads + writes)   0.875 -> 1.143x
  time follows L3 input bytes only (A + B)       0.833 -> 1.200x
  a per-call cost that halving B alone removes   ~1.24x again (what 64/128/64 showed)
Caution already visible in the anchors: 128/64/64 moved 64 MiB in 3,667.9 us (18.3 GB/s)
where 64/128/64 moved 80 MiB in 3,540.7 us (23.7 GB/s), so a single fixed L3 bandwidth does
not describe both tiles even for int8. Arms: upstream, i8:default, unpack:unroll2,
native:unroll2, C single-buffered (L1 60,672 B int8 / 56,576 B int4, both predicted to fit).

ADDENDUM 2, written after the 128/64/64 sweep and before any 64/64/64 run
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
128/64/64 gave int4 nothing (unpack 0.971x, native 1.015x, i8:default 1.016x): none of the
three predictions above held. The two tiles differ in both m and k, so 64/64/64 completes
the 2 x 2. At 64/64/64, 2048^3: B re-streamed M/(4m) = 8 times as at 64/128/64 (A 32 MiB,
B 32 MiB int8 / 16 MiB int4, C 16 MiB: total bytes 0.800 again), while per call B is 4 KB
int8 / 2 KB int4 and A's rows are 64 B contiguous, as at 128/64/64. So:
  int4 gains ~1.24x  -> the effect follows m (B's L3 re-stream count), not k
  int4 gains nothing -> the effect follows k (calls per output tile, A's 128 B rows), not m
L1 (C single): 35,712 B int8, 31,616 B int4 -- both fit. Same four arms, 3 reps.

(Note added after the runs: addendum 2's L1 figures, 35,712 / 31,616 B, are a hand-arithmetic
slip. The design's own l1_estimate printed 36,096 / 32,000 B -- 2 x 4,096 A + 2 x 4,096 (int8)
or 2 x 2,048 (int4) B + 16,384 C + 3,328 stack -- and both fit either way.)
